Alright - so just to be clear, if I make sure that the QTL aren't linked (e.g. not on the same arm of a chromosome), it should be OK to assume a null hypothesis of no QTL when testing for a second using addqtl?

On Friday, August 7, 2015 at 3:54:10 PM UTC-5, Karl Broman wrote:
> Including the genotypes doesn't quite work, as they end up being made independent of the rest of the genome. My approach has been to use the null hypothesis of no QTL as an approximation of the distribution in the presence of a QTL. With linked QTL, this won't work.
>
> Two alternatives:
>
>  - a parametric bootstrap (simulate data under the fitted single-QTL model)
>  - a stratified permutation test (permute the phenotypes within each stratum defined by the genotype at the first QTL)
>
> karl
>
> > On Aug 7, 2015, at 3:40 PM, John Lovell <johnt...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> > Thanks Karl,
> > This is exactly how I am going about scanning for a single QTL.
> > However, I then want to look at the significance of addqtl scans controlling for a single QTL (with or without a covariate interaction). This is why I was putting in the genotypes as a covariate. Should I not do this?
> > John
> >
> > On Friday, August 7, 2015 at 12:08:16 PM UTC-5, Karl Broman wrote:
> > My approach is to do permutations just with the covariate (no QTL genotypes in the addcovar data frame).
> >
> > Let's add model 0: Y ~ Q1 + cover
> >
