Thank you for your reply, Karl. As you have mentioned, I got the linkage groups much more than the chromosomes . After I removed these groups with only 1~2 markers and did swithAllels some times, I got same number of linkage groups with chromosome.

I ordered all the groups and found all of them are very large, and ten of them are larger than 1000cm. I guess that grouping these markers in one group should be correct, but the distance among them must be wrong. But I don't know how to fixed it .........

BTW, I used the map type by four-way.

On Thursday, July 30, 2015 at 8:22:34 PM UTC+8, Karl Broman wrote:
> In my experience, it's unlikely that you'll get the same number of linkage groups as there are chromosomes without making any mistakes. I generally start with more linkage groups than there are chromosomes and merge some later.
>
> The large 500 cM gap in a linkage group likely indicates that that group should be split into two.
>
> With orderMarkers(), the defaults for map.function, maxit, and tol, are probably fine.
> If you want to speed it up, start with making the 'window' argument smaller. Making 'maxit' smaller or 'tol' bigger will also speed it up.
>
> karl
>
>

